.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd August 1, 2026
.Dt VIRTIOFSD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtiofsd
.Nd capability-confined non-DAX virtio-fs backend for bhyve
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl r Ar export
.Fl s Ar socket
.Op Fl U Ar uid
.Op Fl G Ar gid
.Op Fl n Ar nodes
.Op Fl h Ar handles
.Op Fl i Ar inflight
.Op Fl m Ar message-bytes
.Op Fl b Ar pending-bytes
.Op Fl w Ar workers
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
serves a directory through the private, versioned VFSB
.Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET
protocol used by
.Xr bhyve 8 .
It opens the export and listening socket before entering capability mode.
Before entering, it limits each retained descriptor to the rights needed for
read-only export traversal, authenticated socket transport, removal of its
owned socket, or signal-event delivery.
All guest pathname operations are single-component, descriptor-relative
lookups constrained beneath the pre-opened export.
Peer credentials must match the configured user and group.
.Pp
The current implementation is deliberately read-only and non-DAX.
It supports FUSE initialization, lookup, attribute and filesystem-statistics
queries, access checks, symbolic-link reads, file and directory open, file
read, directory enumeration, file flush and sync, single and batched forget,
typed release, cancellation, and backend quiesce/thaw.
Directory enumeration exposes regular files, directories, and symbolic links;
host device nodes, sockets, and FIFOs are omitted.
Unsupported mutating FUSE operations return
.Er EROFS
or
.Er ENOSYS .
Backend state transfer uses versioned, bounded, little-endian records.
The original fixed-size record represents an idle connection.
When nodes or handles are live, a variable-size record preserves their exact
opaque guest identifiers and relative identities beneath the export.
Restore validates and reopens the complete replacement graph before publishing
any of it, and an open handle remains reconstructible after its node lookup is
forgotten.
No host descriptor, pointer, lock, native structure, host byte order, host page
size, or architecture-specific state is serialized.
Regular-file size and type are revalidated; file contents remain an external
read-only export responsibility.
.Dv FUSE_DESTROY
reclaims all handles and non-root lookup nodes even when the guest omitted
individual forget requests.
.Pp
The daemon remains in the foreground for supervision.
It refuses to replace an existing socket path.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r Ar export
Directory to export.
.It Fl s Ar socket
Absolute VFSB listening socket path.
.It Fl U Ar uid
Required effective user ID of the connecting bhyve process.
.It Fl G Ar gid
Required effective group ID of the connecting bhyve process.
.It Fl n Ar nodes
Maximum tracked FUSE nodes.
.It Fl h Ar handles
Maximum open file handles.
.It Fl i Ar inflight
Maximum backend requests.
.It Fl m Ar message-bytes
Maximum FUSE request or response size.
.It Fl b Ar pending-bytes
Maximum queued response payload bytes.
.It Fl w Ar workers
Number of filesystem workers.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr bhyve 8
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The export descriptor, listening socket, and socket-parent descriptor are the
only namespace capabilities retained after startup.
Symlinks are visible as objects but are never followed while resolving or
reopening nodes.
Special files are not exported, and encoded device identifiers are always
zero.
Wire byte order, structure layout, reserved fields, Linux FUSE errno values,
and guest open flags are decoded explicitly rather than using host-native
structures or constants.
